/**
 * Curated checklists for the audit's THREE mutually exclusive run paths
 * (see scoring.ts's countedCategories() for the exact routing), every one
 * of them exactly 50 items:
 *
 *   - Visitor has BOTH GA4 and GTM  → 25 GA4 + 25 GTM = 50
 *   - Visitor has exactly ONE of them → 25 (that tool) + 25 (website-code
 *     core, WEBSITE_CODE_CHECKLIST below) = 50
 *   - Visitor has NEITHER            → 50 Website = 50
 *
 * PageSpeed used to be its own 4-point category (PS-1..PS-4), backed by a
 * site-wide PageSpeed Insights sweep. Removed entirely (2026-09-09): the
 * sweep dominated every run's wall-clock time — a sequential mobile+desktop
 * PSI call per discovered page, minutes of it before claude -p even
 * started. The four things it actually scored are now WEB-60..WEB-63,
 * measured from the headless browser's own network/resource data instead,
 * and every other point that used to lean on a Lighthouse audit id now
 * names the browser or static-crawl check that replaces it.
 *
 * Scoring is flat 1 point per item (fail it, lose the point — see
 * scoring.ts), so the checklist size IS the point scale, not a separate
 * weighted system layered on top of it. A run only ever sends ONE path's
 * checklists to claude -p, never a mix beyond what's listed above — the
 * other categories simply don't run that audit at all (this also keeps the
 * prompt well under the OS argv length limit that a combined 114-item
 * version used to hit).
 *
 * GA4_CHECKLIST was rebuilt from scratch against this worker's ACTUAL tool
 * access, not against a generic human-auditor methodology. The original
 * 203-point Master Tracking Audit Framework (`.claude/skills/ga4-gtm-audit`)
 * was written for a human clicking through the GA4/GTM web UI — Preview
 * mode, DebugView, the Template Gallery permissions tab, a backend/ERP
 * system, a client's own KPI doc. None of that exists for a non-interactive
 * `claude -p` run. Every point here was checked against what `ga4-admin-mcp`
 * (full GA4 Admin v1beta/v1alpha + Data API, including the
 * `admin_request`/`data_request` raw passthroughs — not just its ~30
 * dedicated wrapper tools) can actually answer. A point that needed
 * something that tool doesn't expose — live Preview/DebugView, who has
 * Publish access, a shared-link audit trail, a backend finance system, a
 * client's KPI document — was dropped and replaced with an equally real
 * point that IS answerable this way. `validate` on every point below names
 * the actual MCP tool(s) to call, not a UI path to click through — this
 * worker is an LLM with tool access, not a person with a mouse.
 *
 * GA4_CHECKLIST was rewritten a second time (2026-09-06): 15 of its points
 * (the ones that read real report data — former ids GA4-7, GA4-9, GA4-11,
 * GA4-14 through GA4-25) were replaced by GA4-D1 through GA4-D15, open
 * data-driven analysis slots instead of individually pre-specified checks.
 * A fixed "check this exact metric" list caps what gets found at whatever
 * was anticipated when the checklist was written; these 15 slots instead
 * tell Claude to genuinely dig through everything it can pull from the
 * property and surface up to 15 distinct, high-value business insights on
 * its own — see the GA4-D1..GA4-D15 entries' own comment further down and
 * audit-prompt.ts's "GA4 deep-analysis slots" section for the actual
 * instructions. GA4-1 through GA4-6, GA4-8, GA4-10, GA4-12, and GA4-13 stay
 * fixed checks (config hygiene + the two baseline data-health points) — the
 * category still totals exactly 25 points either way.
 *
 * GTM_CHECKLIST (below) is standardized against "GTM Container Audit
 * Checklist: Top 25 High-Impact Checks" (prepared by Gemini Notebook) — see
 * that list's own doc comment further down for how it was adapted to this
 * worker's actual GTM MCP access (a FIXED tool set: accounts/containers/
 * workspaces/workspace-status, tags/triggers/variables CRUD, built-in
 * variables, versions/live-version/publish — no generic passthrough, no
 * permissions/access-binding endpoint, no template-gallery endpoint, no
 * Preview/DebugView).
 *
 * WEBSITE_CHECKLIST (further below) runs on the OTHER two paths — the full
 * 50 items when the visitor has neither GA4 nor GTM, the 25-item
 * WEBSITE_CODE_CHECKLIST slice when they have exactly one. Both of those
 * paths get a real headless browser (chrome-devtools MCP) on top of the
 * static crawl, which is what every point in the list is scoped to now
 * that the PageSpeed sweep is gone.
 */

export interface ChecklistPoint {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  expectedState: string;
  commonFailure: string;
  // How to actually confirm this point one way or the other — not generic
  // advice, the specific check/report/panel to look at. Some validate steps
  // name tools or data this worker structurally doesn't have access to this
  // run (BigQuery, an ad platform's own Events Manager, Screaming Frog); the
  // prompt is told explicitly which access it actually has each run, and to
  // fall back to whatever signal it DOES have (crawl, PageSpeed, general
  // best practice) to still reach a real pass/fail conclusion when the
  // primary validate step's data isn't available — every point always
  // counts toward this run's 50-check total, there is no silent skip any
  // more (see audit-prompt.ts's buildPrompt).
  validate: string;
  severity: "critical" | "medium" | "low";
}

